(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-dermatology-ii-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Dermatology II Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 19-year-old man reports excessive sweating of both palms and soles since early adolescence, enough to smudge his handwriting. It never occurs while he is asleep. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Secondary generalised hyperhidrosis","Frey syndrome producing gustatory sweating","Autonomic neuropathy from undiagnosed diabetes","Primary focal hyperhidrosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — bilateral, focal, adolescent onset and absent during sleep are the defining features. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A patient with pyoderma gangrenosum asks why the wound clinic will not debride the ulcer as they would for other wounds. Which is the most appropriate explanation?","choices":["The ulcer worsens with trauma, a phenomenon called pathergy, so debridement makes it larger","The ulcer is infected and debridement would spread bacteria into the bloodstream","The ulcer will heal faster if left completely undisturbed by any dressing","Debridement is deferred only until systemic immunosuppression has taken effect"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — this is the single most consequential management point in the condition. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man has recurrent episodes of lip and periorbital swelling without any wheals. Which is the most appropriate initial testing?","choices":["Thyroid-stimulating hormone with anti-thyroid peroxidase antibodies","Complement C4 with C1-esterase inhibitor level and function","Serum tryptase with a search for Darier's sign on examination","Autologous serum skin test for autoimmune chronic urticaria"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — angio-oedema without wheals should raise hereditary angio-oedema. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man with no obesity develops widespread velvety hyperpigmentation over eight weeks, involving the axillae, the oral mucosa and the palms, which have a thickened rugose appearance. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Begin metformin to reduce insulin resistance and reassess in three months","Begin topical retinoids for cosmetic improvement of the affected areas","Reassure her that the change is cosmetic and arrange routine follow-up with lifestyle advice in six months","Urgent evaluation for an underlying malignancy, particularly of the gastrointestinal tract"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — rapid onset, extensive involvement, mucosal change and tripe palms indicate malignant acanthosis nigricans. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has an actinic keratosis on the cheek that has become indurated, tender and has bled twice. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Apply liquid nitrogen with two to three freeze-thaw cycles","Begin imiquimod 5% twice weekly for a sixteen-week course","Begin diclofenac 3% gel and reassess it in three months","Biopsy the lesion to exclude invasive squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — induration, tenderness and bleeding are the concerning features named for progression. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"A 19-year-old man has extensive blistering sunburn over his back and chest with fever, nausea and tachycardia after a day on the water. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Discharge with oral ibuprofen and aloe vera gel, reviewing in one week","Start oral antibiotics to prevent secondary infection of the blistered skin","Admit for intravenous fluids and wound care given the systemic toxicity","Deroof the blisters in clinic and dress the base with a non-adherent layer"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — severe blistering above 20% of the body surface with systemic symptoms warrants hospitalisation. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 74-year-old farmer has deep coarse wrinkles, mottled pigmentation, telangiectasias and thick furrowed skin on the back of his neck. His sun-protected skin looks far younger. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acquired cutis laxa involving the neck","Dermatoheliosis, with cutis rhomboidalis nuchae","Intrinsic chronological aging of the skin","Poikiloderma of Civatte of the lateral neck"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the furrowed posterior neck is the classic marker of severe photoaging. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 117"},{"q":"An infant has generalised blistering from minimal trauma and the subtype is unclear. Which test determines the level of cleavage?","choices":["Serum enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay for structural antibodies","Bacterial culture of blister fluid with sensitivity testing","Skin biopsy with transmission electron microscopy","Skin biopsy with direct immunofluorescence for immunoglobulin G"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the gold standard, with immunofluorescence antigen mapping localising the missing protein. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 25-year-old man with erythema nodosum has no bowel or respiratory symptoms and no recent drug exposure. Which initial investigations should be ordered?","choices":["Antistreptolysin O titre, throat culture, chest radiograph and inflammatory markers","Serum protein electrophoresis, antinuclear and antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies","Complement C4, C1-esterase inhibitor function and serum tryptase","Fasting insulin, haemoglobin A1c and a full lipid profile"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with tuberculin or interferon gamma testing and a pregnancy test where relevant. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has hundreds of small papules over the trunk and limbs, present for a year and mildly itchy. Biopsy confirms granuloma annulare. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Screen for diabetes, thyroid disease and dyslipidaemia, with age-appropriate malignancy screening","Reassure that the condition is benign and arrange review in two years","Begin intralesional triamcinolone to each of the affected lesions","Begin oral terbinafine on the assumption of a widespread dermatophyte infection of the trunk and limbs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — generalised disease in an adult over fifty carries these associations, including lymphoma. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman has a clinically typical polymorphous light eruption. Which investigation is described as mandatory before the diagnosis is accepted?","choices":["An antinuclear antibody panel including anti-Ro and anti-La","A urine, stool and red-cell porphyrin screen sent together","Photopatch testing to a standard series of photoallergens","A skin biopsy of a fresh papule taken within the first day"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — lupus must be excluded because it can present identically. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has a painful expanding leg ulcer and pyoderma gangrenosum is suspected. Which is the most appropriate diagnostic approach?","choices":["Order an ankle-brachial index and venous duplex ultrasound only","Start empirical antibiotics and reassess the ulcer in two weeks","Biopsy the ulcer edge and take wound cultures, since the diagnosis is one of exclusion","Take a punch biopsy from the centre of the ulcer for direct immunofluorescence"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — there is no gold standard test, so malignancy and infection must be excluded first. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man has several uniformly tan, well-demarcated macules 5 to 12 mm across on the dorsal hands and forearms, with no scale or induration. Dermoscopy shows finger-like projections and a moth-eaten border. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Seborrheic keratosis","Pigmented actinic keratosis","Solar lentigo","Lentigo maligna"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — uniform pigment on chronically exposed skin with those dermoscopic features. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"A 17-year-old girl with acanthosis nigricans is embarrassed and has been scrubbing her neck daily, believing it is dirt. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The change is a metabolic warning sign rather than a hygiene problem, and 5 to 10% weight loss improves it","The change is caused by friction from clothing against the neck and will settle with softer, looser fabrics","The change is permanent and no intervention will alter its appearance at all","The change indicates an underlying cancer in most adolescent patients"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the hygiene misconception is specifically addressed in the teaching. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman is admitted with toxic epidermal necrolysis. She has a known lymphoma, a heart rate of 132, detachment of 35% of her body surface, blood urea nitrogen of 34 mg/dL, bicarbonate of 17 mEq/L and glucose of 190 mg/dL. Which score should be calculated and what does it indicate?","choices":["A SCORTEN of 2, indicating a predicted mortality of about 12%","A SCORTEN of 7, indicating a predicted mortality of about 90%","A SCORTEN of 6, indicating a predicted mortality of about 90%","A SCORTEN of 4, indicating a predicted mortality of about 35%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — age, malignancy, heart rate, detachment, urea and bicarbonate each score, and glucose is below threshold. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 9-year-old girl has a ring of small flesh-coloured bumps on the back of her hand, present for four months and not itchy. There is no scale on the border. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tinea corporis of the dorsal hand","Necrobiosis lipoidica of early onset","Subacute cutaneous lupus erythematosus","Localised granuloma annulare"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the absent scale is what separates it from tinea, and localised disease predominates in children. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman with Crohn's disease has biopsy-supported pyoderma gangrenosum that continues to enlarge on high-dose prednisone. Which is the most appropriate next treatment?","choices":["Infliximab at 5 mg/kg intravenously","Surgical debridement of the ulcer bed followed by grafting","Omalizumab at 300 mg subcutaneously every four weeks","Topical high-potency corticosteroid applied to the ulcer base"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is approved for pyoderma gangrenosum in inflammatory bowel disease and is the biologic of choice. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"A 22-year-old man develops widespread wheals, lip swelling and audible wheeze twenty minutes after eating shellfish. Which is the most appropriate immediate treatment?","choices":["Intravenous diphenhydramine followed by oral cetirizine","Oral prednisone 40 to 60 mg as a single loading dose","Nebulised salbutamol with high-flow supplemental oxygen","Intramuscular epinephrine 0.3 mg"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — bronchospasm with angio-oedema is anaphylaxis, and epinephrine comes before anything else. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has tender red lumps on both shins that appeared over a week, preceded by fever and joint aches. Examination shows bilateral tender nodules 2 to 4 cm on the anterior tibial surfaces; none is ulcerated. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cellulitis of both lower legs","Superficial thrombophlebitis of the long saphenous vein","Erythema nodosum","Nodular vasculitis, also called erythema induratum"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — bilateral tender anterior shin nodules that do not ulcerate, after a prodrome of fever and arthralgia. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man has a two-day history of a rash on his hands and forearms. Examination shows symmetric lesions on the palms and dorsal hands, each with a dusky centre, a pale swollen ring and an outer red halo. He had a cold sore ten days ago. There is no mucosal involvement. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute urticaria with an unusual annular pattern","Secondary syphilis involving the palms and soles","Erythema multiforme minor","Stevens-Johnson syndrome in its early phase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Three-zone target lesions on acral surfaces after herpes simplex, with skin involvement only. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has had polymorphous light eruption ruin the first weeks of every summer for four years. It is now February. Which is the most effective preventive strategy?","choices":["Moderate-potency topical corticosteroid applied to the areas each spring","Prophylactic narrowband ultraviolet B, three times weekly for five weeks in spring","Hydroxychloroquine at 200 mg twice daily started at the first eruption","A short course of oral prednisolone taken at the onset of each episode"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it induces tolerance and is named as the most effective preventive strategy. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 46-year-old fair-skinned woman has eighteen months of central facial redness with visible fine vessels across both cheeks and the nose. She flushes with red wine and hot drinks. There are no papules, pustules or comedones. Which subtype is this?","choices":["Acne vulgaris of adult onset","Erythematotelangiectatic rosacea","Papulopustular rosacea in its early phase","Phymatous rosacea affecting the nose"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — persistent central erythema, flushing and telangiectasias without papules or pustules, and the most common subtype. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman on amiodarone develops photosensitivity, and the cardiologist cannot substitute the drug. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Strict photoprotection with broad-spectrum SPF 50 and physical blockers","Prophylactic narrowband ultraviolet B phototherapy given each spring","Oral hydroxychloroquine at 200 mg twice daily as a long-term measure","Oral antihistamines taken each morning through the summer months"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — zinc oxide and titanium dioxide are named where the agent cannot be withdrawn. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 27-year-old man is considering botulinum toxin for axillary hyperhidrosis. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["It commonly causes dry mouth and urinary retention as side effects","It is effective for three to six months, so retreatment will be needed","It permanently destroys the sweat glands in the treated area","It carries a risk of compensatory sweating elsewhere in over 30%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — setting that expectation is specifically named in the patient education. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has itchy wheals that persist for two to three days and fade leaving brownish discoloration. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Increase the second-generation antihistamine to four times the standard licensed daily dose","Measure complement C4 and C1-esterase inhibitor level and function","Begin omalizumab at 300 mg subcutaneously every four weeks","Skin biopsy, since lesions persisting beyond twenty-four hours suggest urticarial vasculitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the persistence and residual bruising are what take this out of simple urticaria. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man with newly confirmed dermatitis herpetiformis is about to start dapsone. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Perform HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8 typing to confirm susceptibility","Check glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase status before the first dose","Check thyroid-stimulating hormone and anti-thyroid peroxidase antibodies","Arrange small bowel biopsy before any treatment is commenced"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, then monitor the blood count for haemolytic anaemia and methaemoglobinaemia. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman with resolved phytophotodermatitis is distressed by persistent brown streaks on her arms. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The pigmentation is permanent and only laser resurfacing will remove it","The pigmentation indicates ongoing inflammation requiring potent steroids","The pigmentation signals a risk of melanoma at those sites in future","The pigmentation fades over months, and daily sunscreen prevents it darkening further"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — reassurance is first line, with hydroquinone or azelaic acid if it persists. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman with suspected dermatitis herpetiformis is to have a biopsy. Which specimen and test confirm the diagnosis?","choices":["Serum sample for immunoglobulin A anti-endomysial antibody testing","Perilesional skin for direct immunofluorescence, seeking granular immunoglobulin A in the dermal papillae","Lesional skin for haematoxylin and eosin, seeking subepidermal blistering with neutrophils","Small bowel mucosa on endoscopy, seeking villous atrophy of the duodenum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — this is the gold standard, and perilesional rather than lesional skin is the key detail. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A neonate develops blisters over the hands and feet after routine handling and nappy changes. There is no fever and the mother is well. Family history includes a father with similar childhood blistering that healed without scars. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Epidermolysis bullosa simplex","Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome","Junctional epidermolysis bullosa of the Herlitz subtype","Bullous pemphigoid of the neonatal period"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — localised to palms and soles, healing without scarring, and autosomal dominant. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman with painful skin and oral erosions has suspected Stevens-Johnson syndrome. Which diagnostic test is considered pathognomonic?","choices":["Serum tryptase measured within four hours of the acute eruption","Punch biopsy showing full-thickness epidermal necrosis with junctional separation","Direct immunofluorescence showing granular immunoglobulin A at the papillae","Transmission electron microscopy with immunofluorescence antigen mapping"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — that histology is the pathognomonic finding named for this condition. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 82"}]};
